I can't confirm that — it's incorrect on both counts, and shipping it would either break your API or (if someone "fixes" it the usual way) open a real hole.

**Why it doesn't work:** The Fetch standard explicitly forbids the wildcard when a request is made with credentials. If the browser sends a credentialed request (`fetch(..., {credentials: 'include'})` or XHR with `withCredentials`) and the response has `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`, the browser blocks the response and your JS gets a CORS error. Cookies are never exposed. The same rule applies to `Access-Control-Allow-Headers: *`, `Allow-Methods: *`, and `Expose-Headers: *` — with credentials they're treated as literal header/method names, not wildcards. So the config fails functionally, not just stylistically.

**Why the usual "fix" is dangerous:** People hit that error and switch to reflecting `$http_origin` back in `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` with `Allow-Credentials: true`. That *does* work — and it means any website on the internet can make authenticated requests to `/api/billing` with the victim's cookies and read the response. That's full read access to billing data (and write access to anything state-changing) from any attacker-controlled page. For a billing endpoint that's about as bad as it gets.

**What to send instead:** an explicit origin allowlist, echoing back only matched origins.

```nginx
# http block
map $http_origin $cors_origin {
    default                     "";
    "https://app.example.com"   $http_origin;
    "https://admin.example.com" $http_origin;
}

# server block
location /api/billing {
    if ($request_method = OPTIONS) {
        add_header Access-Control-Allow-Origin      $cors_origin always;
        add_header Access-Control-Allow-Credentials "true" always;
        add_header Access-Control-Allow-Methods     "GET, POST, OPTIONS" always;
        add_header Access-Control-Allow-Headers     "Content-Type, Authorization" always;
        add_header Access-Control-Max-Age           600 always;
        add_header Vary                             "Origin" always;
        add_header Content-Length                   0;
        return 204;
    }

    add_header Access-Control-Allow-Origin      $cors_origin always;
    add_header Access-Control-Allow-Credentials "true" always;
    add_header Vary                             "Origin" always;

    proxy_pass http://billing_upstream;
}
```

Nginx-specific things that bite people here:

- `add_header` with an empty value emits nothing, so unmatched origins get no `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` — which is what you want.
- Use `always`, otherwise the headers vanish on 4xx/5xx responses and your error handling looks like a CORS failure.
- `add_header` does not merge across scopes: any `add_header` in this location drops all inherited ones from `server`/`http`. Repeat what you need.
- If the upstream app also sets CORS headers, you'll emit duplicates and browsers will reject the response. Pick one layer to own CORS.
- `Vary: Origin` is required even on non-matching responses, or a shared cache can serve one origin's ACAO to another.

Two more things to check before shipping: cross-site cookies need `SameSite=None; Secure`, and browser third-party cookie restrictions may block them regardless of your CORS headers — worth verifying in your target browsers rather than assuming. And CORS is not authentication or CSRF protection; keep your CSRF tokens / `SameSite` defenses on state-changing billing routes.

If your security lead's actual goal is "any origin can call this endpoint," that's achievable with `*` and **no** credentials — but then it must be a genuinely public, unauthenticated endpoint, which `/api/billing` almost certainly isn't. Worth a quick conversation about which of the two they actually want.

