## Why it fails

The CORS check in Fetch: if the request's credentials mode is `include` and `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` is `*`, the response is blocked. Same for `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: *` — it must be the literal string `true`. Your lead's config produces a hard CORS error in every browser, not working cross-origin cookies.

The "fix" people usually reach for next is worse: reflecting `$http_origin` back with `Allow-Credentials: true`. That *does* work — and it means **any website on the internet can read your users' billing data with their cookies**. That's a real vulnerability, not standard CORS.

## Correct config: explicit allowlist

```nginx
# http block
map $http_origin $cors_origin {
    default                     "";
    "https://app.example.com"   $http_origin;
    "https://admin.example.com" $http_origin;
}

# location block
location /api/billing {
    if ($request_method = OPTIONS) {
        add_header Access-Control-Allow-Origin      $cors_origin always;
        add_header Access-Control-Allow-Credentials "true" always;
        add_header Access-Control-Allow-Methods     "GET, POST, OPTIONS" always;
        add_header Access-Control-Allow-Headers     "Content-Type, Authorization" always;
        add_header Access-Control-Max-Age           600 always;
        add_header Vary                             Origin always;
        add_header Content-Length                   0 always;
        return 204;
    }

    add_header Access-Control-Allow-Origin      $cors_origin always;
    add_header Access-Control-Allow-Credentials "true" always;
    add_header Vary                             Origin always;

    proxy_hide_header Access-Control-Allow-Origin;      # avoid duplicates
    proxy_hide_header Access-Control-Allow-Credentials;
    proxy_pass http://billing_upstream;
}
```

Nginx skips `add_header` when the value is empty, so unlisted origins get no CORS headers and the browser blocks them. `always` is required or headers vanish on 4xx/5xx. `Vary: Origin` is mandatory if anything caches these responses.

## Three other things that will bite you

1. **Cookies need `SameSite=None; Secure`** or the browser won't send them cross-site at all, regardless of CORS.
2. **Client must opt in**: `fetch(..., {credentials: 'include'})` or `xhr.withCredentials = true`.
3. **CORS is not CSRF protection.** If `/api/billing` mutates state, you need CSRF tokens or strict `SameSite` — an allowlisted origin doesn't help against a form POST.

If the actual requirement is "any origin can call billing," drop cookies and use a bearer token with `ACAO: *` and no `Allow-Credentials`. That's the only safe way to serve arbitrary origins.

